nodecode-telegram-notify is a rewrite of madpower2000's madpower2000/hermes-telegram-notify:

  https://github.com/madpower2000/hermes-telegram-notify
  commit d7cab7dfb154425118129b0086032e873a832576
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Hermes Agent community (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the Bot API exchange itself — one HTTPS POST to
https://api.telegram.org/bot<token>/sendMessage with a token and a chat id, the
4096-character ceiling, the JSON body, and the API's own words for a chat it
cannot reach — as one tool the model calls, one /telegram-notify command that
says what a message would use, sends a test one and lists the chats the bot has
heard from (the original's discover-chat-id), and a manual topic
(help :telegram-notify) in place of the plugin's lifecycle prompts. The
lifecycle messages are kept and now read off the organism's own frames: a turn
that started, a turn that ended, a turn that failed, and a question waiting
for an answer (the original's approval-request alert), each gated on the event
list the original spelled notify_on_start, notify_on_completion and
notify_on_approval, and each off until the section names it. A turn's end
carries the turn's final answer, bounded by answer_max_chars exactly as the
original bounded it by final_response_max_chars, and the duplicate suppression
the original kept in a locked state file is here one claim per turn id, held in
the cell. The Python is Common Lisp: nlk:http for the one HTTPS exchange in
place of urllib, the JSON reader and writer in nlk in place of json, one
section declaration for the settings in place of the environment-variable
ladder, and nlk:define-contract for the tool's arguments, so the schema the
model is shown and the body that runs are one shape. The state store, its
flock, its retention and its 0600 files are gone with the state file: one turn
is one message, and a restart does not need to remember yesterday's.

Left out: the plugin's audit log and its six logger files, its credentials
file (the token is read from the variable the section names), the platform
suppression rules that only made sense beside a Hermes Telegram gateway
(subagent, Telegram-origin sessions and their approval copies), the
HISTORICAL CODEX_* environment aliases, the profile-multiplexing rules for
the chat destination, and the per-surface approval bookkeeping (smart, cli,
transport) — surfaces this harness does not have. The original also released
its approval requests through the core Telegram adapter's in-chat card; here a
question is a frame the cell reads, and the answer stays where the person
answers it.
